  "contradictions": [
    {
      "subject": "[unsaid]",
      "violated": "The player's character (\"you\") speaks — says, asks, promises or confesses something, in quotes or told (\"you tell her that …\").",
      "evidence": "\"Hold on,\" you tell it. \"I'm coming for you.\"",
      "line": 1,
      "kind": "obligation",
      "confidence": "high"
    }
  ]
}
```

{"contradictions": [
  { "subject": "[cost]", "violated": "1. BREACH [cost] The narration tells as paid a cost the player has not chosen yet, to momentum, health, spirit, supply, a companion's health or the vehicle's integrity", "evidence": "your supplies run short and a wound opens along your arm where the console bit you", "line": 1, "kind": "obligation", "confidence": "high" }
]}

Checking the narration against obligation 1 (BREACH [price]): the price the miss offers is the cutter's guns tearing the hull open. The narration states "you pay for the berth in plating and blood," which tells that price as paid, while the player has not yet chosen whether to pay it. That is a breach of the price line.

The narration also says "her guns rake the hull and tear it open." The price line describes the hull being torn open as the price itself, so that clause is part of the same breach rather than a separate one.

The remaining sentences ("She's dead in the ring, drive's gone" and "people are coming out of cover") concern nothing listed in the ledger's place, identity, binding, retraction or standing sections, so they raise no contradictions.

